= Argument =

== Overview ==
An '''argument''' is a structured set of claims in which one or more premises are offered as reasons for a conclusion.

In logic, an argument is not the same thing as a quarrel or dispute.

== Premises and conclusion ==
The '''premises''' are claims offered in support.

The '''conclusion''' is the claim the argument is intended to establish or support.

The same sentence can be a premise in one argument and a conclusion in another.

== Deductive arguments ==
A deductive argument is evaluated primarily for validity and soundness.

If the conclusion follows necessarily from the premises, the argument is valid.

If it is valid and the premises are true, it is sound.

== Nondeductive arguments ==
Inductive and abductive arguments are not usually evaluated by strict deductive validity.

They can instead be evaluated for evidential strength, explanatory quality, probability, relevance, representativeness, or other standards.

== Argument and explanation ==
An argument offers reasons to accept a conclusion.

An explanation aims to show why or how something is the case.

The same set of statements can sometimes function as either depending on context and purpose.

== Argument and rhetoric ==
Arguments can be communicated rhetorically, but rhetorical effectiveness is not the same as logical or evidential strength.

A persuasive argument can be weak, and a strong argument can be presented badly.

